\description{
SRTR formats record missing information in several ways, and the same code
number means different things in different formats: code \code{4} is "Unknown"
in one format, "N/A" in another and "Not tested" in a third. This dataset
classifies each format code that may stand for missing information, so
that code can be handled by what it means.
}
\details{
The classification describes what a code means. It does not say what an
analysis should do with it.

A code is listed when its meaning matches a broad screen (words such as
"unknown", "not done", "N/A" or "pending"), when it is the SAS missing
value \code{.}, or when it is a format's catch-all. Each listed code was then
classified by hand from its meaning in the SRTR data dictionary. A code
the screen picked up that turned out to be an ordinary value is kept, with
type \code{"none"}, so that it is known to have been looked at. Codes that are
not listed did not match the screen.

The types are sRtr's own grouping of the dictionary's meanings, not an
SRTR classification:
\itemize{
\item \code{"unknown"}: the information is not known.
\item \code{"not_applicable"}: the question does not apply to this person.
\item \code{"not_done"}: the test or assessment was not done.
\item \code{"pending"}: a result was awaited.
\item \code{"cannot_disclose"}: the result is withheld.
\item \code{"indeterminate"}: the test was done and gave no clear result.
\item \code{"partial"}: part of the answer is known, such as "Yes, duration
unknown". \code{collapses_to} names the part that is known.
\item \code{"sas_missing"}: the SAS missing value \code{.}, which the format labels
"Missing".
\item \code{"none"}: an ordinary value.
}
